      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine how much Tom's restaurant makes in a week, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the cost of meals and wine for one night.\n2. Multiply the cost for one night by the number of nights they are open in a week.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the cost of meals and wine for one night:\n- Tom's restaurant gets 6 reservations a night.\n- They order 2 meals, and each meal costs $5.\n- They also order a $5 bottle of wine.\n\nThe cost for the meals is:\n\\[ 2 \\text{ meals} \\times \\$5/\\text{meal} = \\$10 \\]\n\nAdding the cost of the wine:\n\\[ \\$10 + \\$5 = \\$15 \\]\n\nSo, the total cost for one night is $15.\n\nNext, we need to calculate the total cost for a week. They are open 2 days a week:\n\\[ \\$15/\\text{night} \\times 2 \\text{ nights/week} = \\$30/\\text{week} \\]\n\nTherefore, the amount Tom's restaurant makes in a week is:\n\\[ \\boxed{30} \\]",
      "boundary_offset": null,
      "retained_tokens": 242,
      "stop_reason": "native_eos",
      "trigger_text": null
    },
    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine the selling price of each watermelon, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the profit made on the sale of the watermelons.\n2. Add the profit to the original cost to find the total selling price.\n3. Divide the total selling price by the number of watermelons to find the selling price per watermelon.\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. Calculate the profit.\nThe profit is 25% of the original cost. The original cost is $80.\n\\[\n\\text{Profit} = 0.25 \\times 80 = 20\n\\]\n\n2. Calculate the total selling price.\nThe total selling price is the sum of the original cost and the profit.\n\\[\n\\text{Total Selling Price} = 80 + 20 = 100\n\\]\n\n3. Calculate the selling price per watermelon.\nThe total selling price of 50 watermelons is $100. Therefore, the selling price per watermelon is:\n\\[\n\\text{Selling Price per Watermelon} = \\frac{100}{50} = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, the selling price of each watermelon is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
      "boundary_offset": null,
      "retained_tokens": 262,
      "stop_reason": "native_eos",
      "trigger_text": null
    },
    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine how old John's youngest son will be in 3 years, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine James's current age:**\n   - John is currently 32 + 19 = 51 years old.\n   - James is currently twice as old as Dora.\n   - Dora will turn 12 in 3 years, so her current age is 12 - 3 = 9 years old.\n   - Therefore, James's current age is 2 \u00d7 9 = 18 years old.\n\n2. **Determine the youngest son's current age:**\n   - John was 32 years old when he had his youngest son.\n   - John is currently 51 years old, so the time elapsed since he was 32 is 51 - 32 = 19 years.\n   - Since the youngest son was born when John was 32, the youngest son is currently 19 years old.\n\n3. **Determine the youngest son's age in 3 years:**\n   - The youngest son is currently 19 years old.\n   - In 3 years, the youngest son will be 19 + 3 = 22 years old.\n\nThus, the youngest son will be \\boxed{22} years old in 3 years.",
      "boundary_offset": null,
      "retained_tokens": 281,
      "stop_reason": "native_eos",
      "trigger_text": null
    },
    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine how many more oranges Ana will have peeled than Jane after an hour, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Convert the time from hours to minutes**: \n   \\[\n   1 \\text{ hour} = 60 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of oranges each can peel in 60 minutes**:\n   - Ana peels oranges at a rate of 3 minutes per orange.\n   - Jane peels oranges at a rate of 4 minutes per orange.\n\n3. **Determine the number of oranges each can peel in 60 minutes**:\n   - **Ana's peeling rate**: \n     \\[\n     \\frac{60 \\text{ minutes}}{3 \\text{ minutes/orange}} = 20 \\text{ oranges}\n     \\]\n   - **Jane's peeling rate**: \n     \\[\n     \\frac{60 \\text{ minutes}}{4 \\text{ minutes/orange}} = 15 \\text{ oranges}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the difference in the number of oranges peeled**:\n   \\[\n   20 \\text{ oranges} - 15 \\text{ oranges} = 5 \\text{ oranges}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, after an hour, Ana will have peeled \\(\\boxed{5}\\) more oranges than Jane.",
      "boundary_offset": null,
      "retained_tokens": 286,
      "stop_reason": "native_eos",
      "trigger_text": null
    },
    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine how much Mark paid in total for the new radiator and the mechanic's labor, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the discount on the radiator:**\n   The original cost of the radiator is $400, and he gets it at 80% off.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 400 \\times 0.80 = 320\n   \\]\n   So, the price he pays for the radiator after the discount is:\n   \\[\n   400 - 320 = 80\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the cost of the mechanic's labor:**\n   The mechanic charges $50 per hour, and it takes 3 hours to install the radiator.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Labor cost} = 50 \\times 3 = 150\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total cost:**\n   Add the cost of the radiator after the discount and the labor cost:\n   \\[\n   80 + 150 = 230\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total amount Mark paid is \\(\\boxed{230}\\).",
      "boundary_offset": null,
      "retained_tokens": 252,
      "stop_reason": "native_eos",
      "trigger_text": null
    },
    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine the number of chicken animals on Farmer Brown's farm, we can set up a system of equations based on the information given. Let's define the variables first:\n\n- Let \\( c \\) be the number of chicken animals.\n- Let \\( o \\) be the number of cow animals.\n\nWe know two things:\n1. The total number of animals is 20.\n2. The total number of legs is 70.\n\nWe can translate these pieces of information into the following equations:\n1. \\( c + o = 20 \\)\n2. \\( 2c + 4o = 70 \\) (since each chicken has 2 legs and each cow has 4 legs)\n\nWe can solve this system of equations step by step. First, we can solve the first equation for \\( c \\):\n\\[ c = 20 - o \\]\n\nNext, we substitute this expression for \\( c \\) into the second equation:\n\\[ 2(20 - o) + 4o = 70 \\]\n\\[ 40 - 2o + 4o = 70 \\]\n\\[ 40 + 2o = 70 \\]\n\\[ 2o = 30 \\]\n\\[ o = 15 \\]\n\nSo, there are 15 cow animals. Now, we substitute \\( o = 15 \\) back into the equation \\( c = 20 - o \\):\n\\[ c = 20 - 15 \\]\n\\[ c = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of chicken animals is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
      "boundary_offset": null,
